# PTF profile manifest: search-at-scale   (Phase 2b — targets perf issue #2516)
# The analog of a DTF tier manifest. Sourced KV by harness/run.sh.
#
# WHAT THIS PROFILE MEASURES
#   #2516 = million-artifact readiness. This profile answers the core scaling
#   question: does SEARCH/QUERY p95 stay FLAT or DEGRADE as the artifact count
#   grows? It seeds the GLOBAL artifacts table to several scales (SCALE_K_VALUES,
#   default 10k / 100k / 500k) and at each scale fires a fixed mix of the read
#   surfaces — /search/quick, /search/advanced (FTS + name-ILIKE + deep page),
#   and repo artifact listing/paging — measuring p50/p95/p99 per scale. The
#   headline is the p95-vs-count curve (linear? cliff?), with db-pool saturation
#   and the pg_stat_statements top queries (Layer C) showing WHERE the time goes.
#
# WHERE THE TIME GOES (recon-grounded, target-backend @1.6.2-rc)
#   quick_search + advanced_search both route through SearchService::search
#   (services/search_service.rs:273). Its predicate is an INLINE functional FTS:
#     to_tsvector('english', a.name||' '||a.path||' '||COALESCE(a.version,''))
#       @@ to_tsquery('english', $1)
#   with NO GIN index on a stored tsvector column (none in migrations/). So every
#   query recomputes to_tsvector for every row == a SEQUENTIAL SCAN, O(N). A
#   separate COUNT(*) for pagination repeats the same full scan. name=*term*
#   maps to a.name ILIKE '%term%' (non-sargable), and deep pages add OFFSET cost.
#   Expect p95 to grow ~linearly with row count and these queries to dominate
#   pg_stat_statements at 500k — the quantified #2516 cliff.
#
# GLOBAL SCALE (why reset-per-iteration): search scans the whole artifacts table
#   (no repo scope), so the scale is the STANDING table size at measurement time.
#   The scenario truncates + re-grows 0 -> K1 -> K2 -> K3 on every iteration so
#   each scale is measured independently and the trimmed-mean combine stays valid.
#   Equal request counts per scale put the union's p95 in the largest-scale
#   bucket, so the harness-guarded aggregate `p95` reflects at-scale search
#   latency; the per-scale breakdown is emitted to stdout + PTF_SCALING_OUT.
#
# RATE LIMIT — REQUIRED INVOCATION: /search has a 300/min per-IP limiter
#   (routes.rs:680). This profile drives search far past it on purpose, so run
#   the harness with the limiter OFF. compose.base.yml reads it from the shell:
#     RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false bash harness/run.sh search-at-scale \
#       --backend-image artifact-keeper-backend:1.6.2-rc --slot 3 --baseline
#   With the limiter ON, every request past the bucket 429s and this profile
#   measures the limiter, not query latency (the scenario pre-flights + warns).
#
# GEN        : bash generator (curl timing of the read mix; needs docker-exec
#              seeding via seed.sh, so k6-in-container is not used here).
# SCENARIO   : the GEN=bash scenario in this dir.
# PROFILES   : DTF storage overlay to reuse. filesystem is enough — search reads
#              the DB, not the object store, so no blobs are seeded.
# THRESHOLDS : baseline-delta budget + noise-floor band file in this dir.
# RUNS/WARMUP: measured repetitions (trimmed-mean combined) + discarded warm-up.
#              Kept modest because each iteration re-seeds up to max(K) rows.
GEN="bash"
SCENARIO="scenario.sh"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
THRESHOLDS="thresholds"
RUNS="3"
WARMUP="1"

# ---- legacy workload knobs (kept so run.sh's fixed export list is satisfied) --
# This profile does NOT do the upload concurrency/size/dedup sweep; these are
# placeholders that keep run.sh's `export CONCURRENCY FORMATS ...` line valid.
# The search-at-scale knobs below are read by the scenario re-sourcing THIS
# manifest (run.sh does not export arbitrary manifest keys).
CONCURRENCY="1"
FORMATS="search-mix"
SWEEP_SIZE_MB="0"
REQUESTS_PER_CELL="0"
BIGFILE_SIZE_MB="0"
BIGFILE_COUNT="0"
DEDUP_COUNT="0"

# ---- search-at-scale knobs (consumed by scenario.sh; env overrides win) ------
# SCALE_K_VALUES        : GLOBAL artifact counts to seed + measure at (ascending).
#                         The largest K is the "at scale" condition the guarded
#                         p95 reflects. Trim for a quick shared-rig check; the
#                         10k/100k/500k default is the #2516 readiness target.
# QUERY_CONCURRENCY     : concurrent query workers firing the mix at each scale.
# REQUESTS_PER_ENDPOINT : requests per endpoint per scale (per iteration) — the
#                         per-scale percentile sample size (x6 endpoints x scales).
# SEED_REPOS            : repos the K rows spread across (round-robin).
# SEARCH_TERM           : the query term (FTS + name-ILIKE). 'pkg' matches the
#                         seeded names; latency is realized regardless of matches
#                         (the seq scan runs either way).
# SEED_SIZE_BYTES       : per-row size_bytes recorded (cosmetic; no blob written).
SCALE_K_VALUES="10000 100000 500000"
QUERY_CONCURRENCY="4"
REQUESTS_PER_ENDPOINT="15"
SEED_REPOS="5"
SEARCH_TERM="pkg"
SEED_SIZE_BYTES="1024"
